# `date-released` and `doi` are deliberately omitted. Nothing has been released
# -- zero tags, zero releases -- and no DOI has been minted, so either field
# would be a fabricated citation. They belong in the first release commit, not
# here. `version` is held equal to `project.version` in pyproject.toml by
# `tests/test_packaging_metadata.py`: it is the one copy of the version that
# distribution metadata cannot reach.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: Obligation Receipts
message: If you use this software, please cite it using this metadata.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Kelly-Reif
    given-names: Chelsea
version: 0.1.0
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/ChelseaKR/obligation-receipts"
url: "https://github.com/ChelseaKR/obligation-receipts"
abstract: >-
  Obligation Receipts turns approved software-acceptance promises into testable
  obligations and deterministic evidence receipts. It does not draft contracts
  or decide what a contract means: an accountable human first converts approved
  source language into an explicit manifest, and the tool then evaluates that
  manifest against bounded local evidence, reporting each obligation as pass,
  fail, missing, review_required, or unverifiable. Missing evidence never
  becomes a failure or a pass. The receipt records what evidence was evaluated
  under which manifest and source digest, and re-verifies offline. It is
  unsigned at this milestone and therefore proves neither authorship nor that a
  contracting authority accepted a deliverable.
keywords:
  - acceptance-testing
  - civic-tech
  - deterministic
  - evidence
  - fail-closed
  - federal-acquisition-regulation
  - government-contracting
  - govtech
  - offline-first
  - provenance
  - public-procurement
  - receipts
  - replay
  - section-508
  - software-acceptance
  - supply-chain-security
